1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,251
The Changi Murals:

2
00:00:01,331 --> 00:00:05,250
Stanley Warren's Chapel
Paintings at Roberts Barracks

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:09,845
In September 1942, in a
prisoner-of-war hospital at Changi,

4
00:00:09,925 --> 00:00:13,570
a British bombardier
named Stanley Warren began

5
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:17,850
painting Bible scenes on
the walls of a small chapel.

6
00:00:17,850 --> 00:00:21,484
He was so ill with dysentery
and kidney disease that he

7
00:00:21,564 --> 00:00:25,063
could only work for ten to
fifteen minutes at a time,

8
00:00:25,143 --> 00:00:29,600
and his paints were scrounged
camouflage paint and billiard chalk.

9
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,338
Within two years his five murals had
vanished under coats of distemper,

10
00:00:34,418 --> 00:00:38,150
and Warren went home believing
they had been destroyed.

11
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,379
They are still there
today, in a building inside

12
00:00:41,459 --> 00:00:44,700
an air force camp that
the public cannot enter.

13
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:49,552
Singapore surrendered to the Japanese
on the 15th of February 1942,

14
00:00:49,632 --> 00:00:53,749
and tens of thousands of
British and Australian prisoners

15
00:00:53,829 --> 00:00:58,025
were marched to Changi, at
the eastern tip of the island.

16
00:00:58,025 --> 00:01:02,151
They were held in and around the
British Army's own barracks there,

17
00:01:02,231 --> 00:01:06,375
and Roberts Barracks was turned
into a hospital for the prisoners.

18
00:01:06,375 --> 00:01:09,615
Stanley Warren had been
a commercial designer

19
00:01:09,695 --> 00:01:13,451
before the war, and had
reached Malaya early in 1942

20
00:01:13,531 --> 00:01:16,925
with the 15th Field
Regiment, Royal Artillery.

21
00:01:16,925 --> 00:01:20,651
On the 23rd of May 1942 he
was sent to the hospital

22
00:01:20,731 --> 00:01:24,457
at Roberts Barracks, and
by the middle of August he

23
00:01:24,537 --> 00:01:27,219
was in the dysentery wing, Block 151,

24
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:32,075
a three-storey building that held
more than a thousand patients.

25
00:01:32,075 --> 00:01:35,052
On its ground floor the
prisoners had made a

26
00:01:35,132 --> 00:01:39,499
small room into a chapel, dedicated
to Saint Luke the Physician,

27
00:01:39,579 --> 00:01:41,525
the patron saint of doctors.

28
00:01:41,525 --> 00:01:45,100
Warren had painted before in captivity.

29
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:49,286
At a camp at Bukit Batok, a
chaplain had asked him to decorate

30
00:01:49,366 --> 00:01:53,700
the chapel there, and he drew
two religious scenes in charcoal.

31
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:57,949
At Roberts Barracks, two chaplains,
Padres Chambers and Payne,

32
00:01:58,029 --> 00:02:01,450
asked him to do the same
for Saint Luke's Chapel.

33
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:06,560
Warren, who had joined the chapel choir
after hearing the hymns from his bed,

34
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,550
agreed as a way of giving
thanks for his partial recovery.

35
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:14,751
He was still working on his
sketches when the Selarang

36
00:02:14,831 --> 00:02:18,875
Barracks incident began on
the 30th of August 1942.

37
00:02:18,875 --> 00:02:22,429
The Japanese ordered the
prisoners to sign a pledge

38
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:26,063
not to escape, and when
almost all of them refused,

39
00:02:26,143 --> 00:02:29,626
nearly 17,000 men were
crowded into and around the

40
00:02:29,706 --> 00:02:33,625
square of Selarang Barracks
until the 5th of September.

41
00:02:33,625 --> 00:02:37,250
Warren began painting in early September.

42
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:40,557
That month the men of his
work party were listed to go

43
00:02:40,637 --> 00:02:44,320
north to the Thai-Burma Railway,
and a colonel stepped in so

44
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:47,975
that he could stay at the
hospital and finish the chapel.

45
00:02:47,975 --> 00:02:52,550
Warren painted five murals,
each about three metres long.

46
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:55,945
In order, they showed the
Nativity, the Ascension,

47
00:02:56,025 --> 00:03:00,125
the Crucifixion, the Last
Supper, and Saint Luke in Prison.

48
00:03:00,125 --> 00:03:04,825
The Nativity was finished
by Christmas 1942.

49
00:03:04,825 --> 00:03:08,489
Paint was almost impossible
to get, so fellow prisoners

50
00:03:08,569 --> 00:03:11,961
found him brown camouflage
paint, a little crimson,

51
00:03:12,041 --> 00:03:16,125
some white oil paint, and
billiard chalk crushed for colour.

52
00:03:16,125 --> 00:03:19,074
With so little to work with, he used broad

53
00:03:19,154 --> 00:03:22,400
brush strokes and large
areas of flat colour.

54
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,239
He never signed the murals,

55
00:03:24,319 --> 00:03:27,375
because he considered
them "a gift to God".

56
00:03:27,375 --> 00:03:30,850
In May 1944, Block 151 was turned

57
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:34,700
into a store for a nearby airfield.

58
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:38,304
A doorway was knocked through
the wall into the next room,

59
00:03:38,384 --> 00:03:42,940
which almost completely destroyed the
lower part of Saint Luke in Prison,

60
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:46,450
and the chapel walls were
covered over with distemper.

61
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:50,120
Warren himself was moved
to a camp at Kranji,

62
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:55,200
where he stayed until the
Japanese surrender in August 1945.

63
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:59,046
He went back to England believing
that the murals had been

64
00:03:59,126 --> 00:04:03,525
destroyed in Allied bombing, and
became an art teacher in London.

65
00:04:03,525 --> 00:04:07,775
After the war, the barracks
became part of RAF Changi.

66
00:04:07,775 --> 00:04:12,396
Some years later, airmen
clearing out an unused storeroom

67
00:04:12,476 --> 00:04:17,425
in Block 151 noticed pictures
under the layers of distemper.

68
00:04:17,425 --> 00:04:20,450
Accounts differ on when this happened.

69
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:26,172
The RAF Changi Association
remembers it as the early 1950s,

70
00:04:26,252 --> 00:04:29,300
while other accounts give 1958.

71
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:33,494
The story reached the Singapore
papers in January 1959,

72
00:04:33,574 --> 00:04:38,235
when the Sunday Times printed
a photograph of one mural under

73
00:04:38,315 --> 00:04:42,900
the headline "Do you know who
painted this PoW camp mural?"

74
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:48,113
The Sunday Times suggested that the
murals might be the work of Ronald Searle,

75
00:04:48,193 --> 00:04:51,925
the cartoonist, who had also
been a prisoner at Changi.

76
00:04:51,925 --> 00:04:56,575
His wife told reporters in London
that he had not painted them.

77
00:04:56,575 --> 00:05:00,601
A reader in the RAF Changi
Education Library then found

78
00:05:00,681 --> 00:05:05,602
Warren's name in a book, "The
Churches of the Captivity in Malaya",

79
00:05:05,682 --> 00:05:08,214
and on the 1st of February 1959 the

80
00:05:08,294 --> 00:05:12,400
Sunday Times reported that
the mystery had been solved.

81
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:16,374
The Daily Mirror in London took
up the search, and that month

82
00:05:16,454 --> 00:05:20,175
Warren was found living in
London with his wife and son.

83
00:05:20,175 --> 00:05:22,907
Once the distemper had been scraped away,

84
00:05:22,987 --> 00:05:27,650
four complete murals and the top
quarter of the fifth could be seen.

85
00:05:27,650 --> 00:05:30,286
In 1960 the RAF asked Warren

86
00:05:30,366 --> 00:05:33,275
to come back and restore them.

87
00:05:33,275 --> 00:05:39,193
He was reluctant at first, because the
work brought back painful memories,

88
00:05:39,273 --> 00:05:42,111
but he made three trips to Singapore

89
00:05:42,191 --> 00:05:46,325
to do it, in December 1963,
July 1982 and May 1988.

90
00:05:46,325 --> 00:05:48,948
On his first visit, the Straits Times

91
00:05:49,028 --> 00:05:51,950
described him as "a man with a mission".

92
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:56,664
The 1982 trip was the most
intensive, and officers and boys

93
00:05:56,744 --> 00:06:01,375
of the SAF Boys' School helped
him with much of the work.

94
00:06:01,375 --> 00:06:04,545
Saint Luke in Prison was
never fully restored,

95
00:06:04,625 --> 00:06:09,075
because Warren could no longer
remember the missing lower part.

96
00:06:09,075 --> 00:06:12,723
In 1985 his original drawing
for it turned up among

97
00:06:12,803 --> 00:06:16,450
the belongings of a fellow
prisoner, Wally Hammond,

98
00:06:16,530 --> 00:06:20,550
and it was given to the
National Archives of Singapore.

99
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:24,305
By 1988 Warren was not fit
enough to repaint the mural,

100
00:06:24,385 --> 00:06:27,373
so he made a small
picture from the drawing,

101
00:06:27,453 --> 00:06:30,800
which was placed beneath
the surviving fragment.

102
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:34,570
Stanley Warren died at
his home in Bridport,

103
00:06:34,650 --> 00:06:39,725
in Dorset, on the 20th of
February 1992, at the age of 75.

104
00:06:39,725 --> 00:06:44,415
In October 1968 a British member
of parliament, Charles Morris,

105
00:06:44,495 --> 00:06:49,034
asked the Defence Secretary,
Denis Healey, to consider moving

106
00:06:49,114 --> 00:06:53,426
the murals to England, but
nothing came of it, and in 1969

107
00:06:53,506 --> 00:06:58,125
Singapore's Ministry of Defence
took responsibility for them.

108
00:06:58,125 --> 00:07:01,760
In the 1990s the National
Heritage Board marked

109
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:05,475
the site as one of
Singapore's historic sites.

110
00:07:05,475 --> 00:07:10,879
Most of the buildings around Block
151 were pulled down in July 2003,

111
00:07:10,959 --> 00:07:15,013
but the block itself still
stands inside what is now

112
00:07:15,093 --> 00:07:20,100
the Changi Air Base camp, and
access to the camp is restricted.

113
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:23,925
Most visitors see the murals as replicas.

114
00:07:23,925 --> 00:07:28,134
The Changi Chapel and Museum,
on Upper Changi Road North,

115
00:07:28,214 --> 00:07:31,144
has shown copies of them for many years,

116
00:07:31,224 --> 00:07:35,508
and the museum reopened in May
2021 after a redevelopment,

117
00:07:35,588 --> 00:07:39,200
with the replicas again
among its main exhibits.

118
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:42,331
A copy of one of the
murals also hangs in the

119
00:07:42,411 --> 00:07:46,050
Garrison Church at Larkhill,
in Wiltshire, England.

120
00:07:46,050 --> 00:07:49,681
Where it fits in the bigger
story: The Changi Murals were

121
00:07:49,761 --> 00:07:53,067
painted by a sick prisoner
with scrounged materials,

122
00:07:53,147 --> 00:07:57,364
hidden for years under distemper,
and traced back to their painter

123
00:07:57,444 --> 00:08:01,025
partly by chance and partly
through a newspaper appeal.

124
00:08:01,025 --> 00:08:04,239
They are still on the walls
where they were painted,

125
00:08:04,319 --> 00:08:08,500
and the man who painted them came
back three times to repair them.

126
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:12,477
Because the originals sit
inside a working military camp,

127
00:08:12,556 --> 00:08:15,252
most Singaporeans know them, if at all,

128
00:08:15,332 --> 00:08:19,175
only through the replicas
at the museum down the road.
